 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T20:24+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, the haze is currently bad in Singapore.** \n\nAir quality has been impacted by transboundary smoke haze driven by fires in Kalimantan and Sumatra:\n\n* **PSI Levels:** The 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) remains elevated, sitting mostly in the **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** ranges (with readings widely ranging between roughly 130 and nearly 200+ depending on the region). \n* **PM2.5 Levels:** One-hour PM2.5 concentrations have pushed into the **high band** in several regions (such as the west, central, and south parts of the island), indicating heavy concentrations of fine particulate matter. \n\n### **Health Advisories & Precautions:**\n* **General Public / Healthy Individuals:** Should avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion. \n* **Elderly, Pregnant Women, and Children:** Advised to minimize outdoor activities. If 1-hour PM2.5 levels enter the high band, you should avoid outdoor activities for that hour and consider wearing a mask if heading out is necessary.\n* **Persons with Chronic Heart/Lung Conditions:** Should avoid outdoor activities altogether and keep medication on hand.\n\nBecause air quality can fluctuate rapidly depending on shifting winds and weather, it is best to check real-time updates directly via the [myENV app](https://www.haze.gov.sg) or local news before heading outdoors.",
